  "backing": {
    "collateral_ratio_scale": "percent",
    "axis_rating_basis": "Rated on the generic band deliberately. The CDP book's liquidation floor IS now measured (backing.alternatives.cdp_liquidation_floor_pct, from each controller's liquidation_discount) but it does not transfer to this axis: it anchors collateral/debt on the CDP book, whereas this ratio is backing over DEPLOYED supply. Anchoring cutoffs on it would rate a figure the dashboard does not display. \u26a0\ufe0f 4/5 on the deployed basis is defensible only because backing.alternatives now shows what that basis excludes \u2014 the full-supply ratio is materially lower, and the exclusion runs in the generous direction.",
    "collateral_ratio_basis": "Deployed basis: backing / (total_supply - total_pegkeeper_debt). PegKeeper-minted crvUSD is protocol-owned inventory, not debt borrowed against collateral, so it is excluded from the denominator. \u26a0\ufe0f That exclusion is 9.6% of supply ($23,513,042) \u2014 not a footnote. Basis changed 2026-08-18; the minted-basis and pro-rata alternatives are in backing.basis, and the full-supply ratio is in backing.alternatives.",
    "collateral_ratio": 117.0,
    "total_backing": 258778922.21,
    "surplus_deficit": 37599154.06,
    "surplus_deficit_basis": "Minting-source collateral value minus crvUSD supply. \u26a0\ufe0f THIS IS NOT A CUSHION OWNED BY crvUSD HOLDERS. The overcollateralization is BORROWERS' EQUITY: a borrower can repay and withdraw their excess collateral at will, so it is capital inside the surplus that can walk out \u2014 the same shape as yzUSD's junior tranche, arrived at by a different mechanism. What protects holders is the LIQUIDATION machinery (soft liquidation progressively converts collateral as prices fall), not ownership of the excess. So a multiple against this figure answers 'how many times the collateral currently posted', never 'how many times the loss-absorbing capital'.",
